            {
              "id": "ed9be015-05d3-3296-9322-4f7980f53f2f",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "This finding is classified as a genuine vulnerability because the analysis identified a risk of undefined behavior due to possible overlap between the source and destination memory regions in the memory copy operation. The destination buffer is a pointer, and the source is a global identifier. Dataflow analysis suggests that the destination may alias the source, which can lead to undefined behavior when using memory copy functions like memcpy. Additionally, there is no evidence of a guard to prevent null pointers for the source, further increasing the risk. The absence of explicit buffer size checks or overlap handling makes this a high-confidence issue that should be addressed to prevent potential memory corruption or crashes.\n\n## In Context Remediation\nTo prevent undefined behavior when the source and destination memory regions may overlap, replace the memory copy operation with a function that safely handles overlapping regions, such as memmove. This ensures that the copy is performed correctly even if the regions overlap.\n\n```cpp\nmemmove(buf, importThunkARM64, sizeof(importThunkARM64));\n```\n\nAdditionally, ensure that both the source and destination pointers are not null before performing the operation:\n\n```cpp\nif (buf != nullptr && importThunkARM64 != nullptr) {\n    memmove(buf, importThunkARM64, sizeof(importThunkARM64));\n}\n```\n\n## References\n- [OWASP C-Based Toolchain Hardening Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-119: Improper Restriction of Operations within the Bounds of a Memory Buffer](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/119.html)\n- [CWE-787: Out-of-bounds Write](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/787.html)\n- [OWASP Buffer Overflow](https://owasp.org/www-community/vulnerabilities/Buffer_Overflow)\n\n___\n"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS121708"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "5583efaf-8a51-3403-913e-6c5f487efb0c",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "This finding is classified as a genuine vulnerability because it is marked as a high-confidence undefined behavior risk due to possible overlap between the source and destination memory regions. The analysis detected that the destination buffer and the source (importThunkARM) may refer to overlapping memory, which can cause undefined behavior when using memcpy. Additionally, there is no evidence of a guard to prevent this overlap, and the source pointer may also be null at this call site, further increasing the risk. The destination buffer is a pointer type with no known capacity, and the size argument is not derived from the destination's capacity. All these factors indicate a real risk of memory corruption or program instability.\n\n## In Context Remediation\nTo prevent undefined behavior when the source and destination memory regions may overlap, use `memmove` instead of `memcpy`. `memmove` is designed to safely handle overlapping memory areas. Replace the original call with:\n\n```cpp\nmemmove(buf, importThunkARM, sizeof(importThunkARM));\n```\n\nAdditionally, ensure that both `buf` and `importThunkARM` are not null before the call to avoid null pointer dereference. If possible, add explicit checks:\n\n```cpp\nif (buf != nullptr && importThunkARM != nullptr) {\n    memmove(buf, importThunkARM, sizeof(importThunkARM));\n}\n```\n\n## References\n- [OWASP C-Based Toolchain Hardening Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-119: Improper Restriction of Operations within the Bounds of a Memory Buffer](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/119.html)\n- [CWE-787: Out-of-bounds Write](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/787.html)\n- [OWASP Buffer Overflow](https://owasp.org/www-community/vulnerabilities/Buffer_Overflow)\n\n___\n"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS121708"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "e13cebab-a197-3d93-bed6-402d0c0cea44",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "This finding is classified as a genuine vulnerability because it is marked as a 'UB Risk' (undefined behavior risk) due to possible overlap between the source and destination memory regions in the memory copy operation. The analysis detected that the destination and source may overlap based on data flow, which can lead to undefined behavior when using functions like memcpy. Additionally, the source pointer may be null at this call site, as there is no prior check ensuring it is non-null. The destination buffer is a pointer type, and there is no evidence that its capacity is being checked or that the length argument is derived from the destination's size. No explicit null-termination or guard is present. All these factors increase the risk of a real issue, and the verdict is 'GENUINE', indicating a high-confidence, actionable problem.\n\n## In Context Remediation\nTo prevent undefined behavior when copying memory where the source and destination may overlap, use `memmove` instead of `memcpy`. `memmove` is designed to handle overlapping regions safely. Replace the original call with:\n\n```cpp\nmemmove(buf, importThunkX86, sizeof(importThunkX86));\n```\n\nAdditionally, ensure that both `buf` and `importThunkX86` are not null before performing the copy to avoid null pointer dereference:\n\n```cpp\nif (buf != nullptr && importThunkX86 != nullptr) {\n    memmove(buf, importThunkX86, sizeof(importThunkX86));\n}\n```\n\n## References\n- [OWASP C-Based Toolchain Hardening Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-119: Improper Restriction of Operations within the Bounds of a Memory Buffer](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/119.html)\n- [CWE-787: Out-of-bounds Write](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/787.html)\n- [OWASP Buffer Overflow](https://owasp.org/www-community/vulnerabilities/Buffer_Overflow)\n\n___\n"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS121708"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "734d4176-f472-3a4e-bbed-36f3fafa7b60",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "This finding is classified as a genuine vulnerability because it is marked as a high-confidence undefined behavior risk due to possible overlap between the source and destination memory regions. The analysis detected that the destination buffer and the source may overlap, which can cause undefined behavior when using memory copy operations like memcpy. Additionally, there is a risk that the source pointer could be null at this point in the code, as there is no prior check ensuring it is non-null. The destination buffer is a pointer type, and there is no evidence that its capacity is being checked or that the length argument is derived from the buffer's actual size. No explicit null-termination is performed after the copy, and there are no guards in place to prevent underflow or overflow. All these factors indicate a genuine and actionable security issue.\n\n## In Context Remediation\nTo prevent undefined behavior when copying memory where the source and destination may overlap, use `memmove` instead of `memcpy`. `memmove` is designed to handle overlapping memory regions safely. Replace the original call with:\n\n```cpp\nmemmove(buf, importThunkX86, sizeof(importThunkX86));\n```\n\nAdditionally, ensure that both `buf` and `importThunkX86` are not null before performing the copy to avoid null pointer dereference. For example:\n\n```cpp\nif (buf != nullptr && importThunkX86 != nullptr) {\n    memmove(buf, importThunkX86, sizeof(importThunkX86));\n}\n```\n\n## References\n- [OWASP C-Based Toolchain Hardening Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-119: Improper Restriction of Operations within the Bounds of a Memory Buffer](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/119.html)\n- [CWE-787: Out-of-bounds Write](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/787.html)\n- [CWE-124: Buffer Underwrite ('Buffer Underflow')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/124.html)\n- [CWE-476: NULL Pointer Dereference](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/476.html)\n- [OWASP Buffer Overflow](https://owasp.org/www-community/vulnerabilities/Buffer_Overflow)\n\n___\n"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS121708"
                ]
              }
