            {
              "id": "9f432ba7-199b-3d94-b819-60156dad6b0d",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "This finding is classified as a genuine vulnerability because the analysis identified a risk of undefined behavior due to possible overlap between the source and destination memory regions in the memory copy operation. The destination and source may refer to overlapping memory, as indicated by the dataflow analysis. Additionally, there is no explicit check to ensure that the source pointer is not null, which could also lead to undefined behavior. The verdict is supported by the high-confidence classification and the explicit 'GENUINE' verdict. The absence of a guard for null pointers and the feasibility of overlap further increase the risk. The destination is not likely to be null, but the source may be, and the overlap is confirmed by dataflow analysis.\n\n## In Context Remediation\nTo prevent undefined behavior when copying memory where the source and destination regions may overlap, use `memmove` instead of `memcpy`. `memmove` is specifically designed to handle overlapping memory regions safely. Replace the original call with:\n\n```cpp\nmemmove(buf + sizeof(addrInst), pacBr, sizeof(pacBr));\n```\n\nAdditionally, ensure that the source pointer (`pacBr`) is not null before performing the copy to avoid null pointer dereference:\n\n```cpp\nif (pacBr != nullptr) {\n    memmove(buf + sizeof(addrInst), pacBr, sizeof(pacBr));\n}\n```\n\n## References\n- [OWASP: Buffer Overflow](https://owasp.org/www-community/vulnerabilities/Buffer_Overflow)\n- [CWE-119: Improper Restriction of Operations within the Bounds of a Memory Buffer](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/119.html)\n- [CWE-787: Out-of-bounds Write](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/787.html)\n- [CWE-676: Use of Potentially Dangerous Function](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/676.html)\n\n___\n"
