            {
              "id": "94f46ffa-57eb-3f1f-9d38-2f223c5be768",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "This finding is considered a genuine vulnerability because the classification indicates a high risk: the destination buffer's capacity is unknown, and the number of bytes being copied is not proven to be safe. The verdict for this call site is also marked as genuine. The destination buffer is a pointer, and there is no information about its allocated size, which means there is a risk of writing beyond its bounds. The source is a string literal of 4 bytes, and the copy length is 4, but without knowing the size of the destination, this operation could cause a buffer overflow if the buffer is too small. There are no explicit checks or guards to ensure the destination is large enough, and there is no evidence that the length is derived from the buffer's capacity. The absence of overlap and null pointer risks does not mitigate the core issue of potential overflow due to unknown buffer size.\n\n## In Context Remediation\nBefore copying data into the destination buffer, ensure that the buffer is large enough to hold the number of bytes being copied. If the buffer's size is not known at this point in the code, pass the buffer size as an additional argument or use a safer function that takes the buffer size into account. For example, if you know the buffer's size is at least 4 bytes, you can add an assertion or a runtime check:\n\n```cpp\nassert(buf != nullptr);\n// Ensure 'buf' points to at least 4 bytes\nmemcpy(buf, \"\\177ELF\", 4);\n```\n\nAlternatively, if the buffer size is variable, pass it as a parameter and check it before copying:\n\n```cpp\nif (buf_size >= 4) {\n    memcpy(buf, \"\\177ELF\", 4);\n} else {\n    // Handle error: buffer too small\n}\n```\n\nIf possible, use `std::copy` or safer functions that require the buffer size, or switch to `std::array` or `std::vector` for automatic bounds management.\n\n## References\n- [OWASP C-Based Toolchain Hardening Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [OWASP Buffer Overflow](https://owasp.org/www-community/vulnerabilities/Buffer_Overflow)\n- [CWE-119: Improper Restriction of Operations within the Bounds of a Memory Buffer](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/119.html)\n- [CWE-120: Buffer Copy without Checking Size of Input ('Classic Buffer Overflow')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/120.html)\n- [CWE-787: Out-of-bounds Write](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/787.html)\n\n___\n"
              },
