            {
              "id": "c57f83f9-0eaa-3323-9b94-a77d88dde435",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "This finding is considered a genuine vulnerability because the classification indicates a high risk: the destination buffer's capacity is unknown, and the number of bytes being copied is not proven to be safe. The destination is a pointer to an unsigned 8-bit integer, and there is no information about its allocated size at this point in the code. The source is a string literal of two bytes, and the copy operation requests exactly two bytes. While the source is safe and there is no risk of overlapping memory or null pointers, the main concern is that the destination buffer may not be large enough to hold the copied data, which could result in a buffer overflow. There are no explicit checks or guards ensuring the destination buffer is at least two bytes in size, and the code does not assign a null terminator after the copy. This pattern is a common source of memory safety vulnerabilities in C/C++ code.\n\n## In Context Remediation\nBefore copying data into the destination buffer, ensure that the buffer is large enough to hold the number of bytes being copied. If the size of the destination buffer is not known at this point in the code, add an explicit check or pass the buffer size as an additional parameter. For example:\n\n```cpp\n// Ensure 'inst' points to a buffer of at least 2 bytes before this call\nif (inst != nullptr /* && buffer_size >= 2 */) {\n    memcpy(inst, \"\\x48\\x8d\", 2);\n}\n```\nIf the buffer size is not available, refactor the code to include it, or use a safer alternative that checks bounds. Never copy data into a buffer of unknown size.\n\n## References\n- [OWASP C-Based Toolchain Hardening Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [OWASP Buffer Overflow](https://owasp.org/www-community/vulnerabilities/Buffer_Overflow)\n- [CWE-119: Improper Restriction of Operations within the Bounds of a Memory Buffer](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/119.html)\n- [CWE-120: Buffer Copy without Checking Size of Input ('Classic Buffer Overflow')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/120.html)\n- [CWE-787: Out-of-bounds Write](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/787.html)\n\n___\n"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS121708"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "2b9e6dfe-bcd2-3669-ad5f-62ec828b7ba7",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "This finding is considered a genuine vulnerability because the classification indicates a high risk: the destination buffer's capacity is unknown, and the number of bytes being copied is not proven to be safe. The destination is a pointer to an unsigned 8-bit integer, and there is no information about its allocated size at this point in the code. The source is a string literal of two bytes, and the copy operation requests exactly two bytes. While the source is safe and there is no risk of overlapping memory or null pointers, the main concern is that the destination buffer may not be large enough to hold the copied data, which could result in a buffer overflow. There are no explicit checks or guards in the code to ensure the destination buffer is large enough. This makes the operation risky and potentially exploitable if the buffer is undersized.\n\n## In Context Remediation\nBefore copying data into the destination buffer, ensure that the buffer is large enough to hold the number of bytes being copied. If the size of the destination buffer is not known, add an explicit check or document the required buffer size in the function's contract. For example, if `inst` is expected to point to a buffer of at least 2 bytes, validate this before the copy:\n\n```cpp\n// Example: Ensure 'inst' points to a buffer of at least 2 bytes before copying\nif (inst != nullptr /* && buffer_size >= 2 */) {\n    memcpy(inst, \"\\x4d\\x8d\", 2);\n} else {\n    // Handle error: insufficient buffer size\n}\n```\n\nIf possible, refactor the code so that the size of the destination buffer is always known and passed explicitly, or use safer alternatives that require the buffer size as an argument.\n\n## References\n- [OWASP: Buffer Overflow](https://owasp.org/www-community/vulnerabilities/Buffer_Overflow)\n- [CWE-119: Improper Restriction of Operations within the Bounds of a Memory Buffer](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/119.html)\n- [CWE-120: Buffer Copy without Checking Size of Input ('Classic Buffer Overflow')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/120.html)\n- [C-Based Toolchain Hardening Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n\n___\n"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS121708"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "f09a1024-c27b-3985-9efa-8d35493f7f10",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "This finding is considered a genuine vulnerability because the classification indicates a high risk due to the destination buffer's capacity being unknown and the copy length not being proven safe. The destination is a pointer to an unsigned 8-bit integer, and there is no information about its allocated size at this point in the code. The source is a fixed string literal of 3 bytes, and the copy length is also 3, which matches the source size. However, since there is no evidence that the destination buffer is at least 3 bytes long, there is a risk of a buffer overflow if the buffer is smaller than expected. There are no explicit checks or guards ensuring the destination is large enough, and the destination is not known to be null or overlapping with the source. The risk is further highlighted by the lack of any explicit null-termination or post-copy checks. Therefore, this is a genuine issue that should be addressed to prevent potential memory corruption or security vulnerabilities.\n\n## In Context Remediation\nBefore copying data into the destination buffer, ensure that the buffer is at least as large as the number of bytes being copied. If the size of the destination buffer is not known, add a check or assert to verify its size. For example, if `inst` is dynamically allocated, ensure it is allocated with at least 3 bytes:\n\n```cpp\n// Example: Ensure inst is allocated with at least 3 bytes\nassert(inst != nullptr);\n// If you know the size, check it before copying\nsize_t inst_size = /* actual size of inst */;\nif (inst_size >= 3) {\n    memcpy(inst, \"\\x49\\x81\\xc4\", 3);\n} else {\n    // Handle error: buffer too small\n}\n```\nIf the buffer is statically allocated, ensure its size is at least 3 bytes:\n\n```cpp\nuint8_t inst[3]; // or larger\nmemcpy(inst, \"\\x49\\x81\\xc4\", 3);\n```\nNever perform the copy if the buffer size is not guaranteed. This prevents buffer overflows and potential security vulnerabilities.\n\n## References\n- [OWASP C-Based Toolchain Hardening Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [OWASP Buffer Overflow](https://owasp.org/www-community/vulnerabilities/Buffer_Overflow)\n- [CWE-119: Improper Restriction of Operations within the Bounds of a Memory Buffer](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/119.html)\n- [CWE-120: Buffer Copy without Checking Size of Input ('Classic Buffer Overflow')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/120.html)\n- [CWE-787: Out-of-bounds Write](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/787.html)\n\n___\n"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS121708"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "94ffc89c-7feb-35e5-a217-4d5b8a92f4f4",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "This finding is considered a genuine vulnerability because the classification indicates a high risk: the destination buffer's capacity is unknown, and the number of bytes being copied is not proven to be safe. The destination is a pointer, and there is no evidence that its size is sufficient to hold the copied data. Although the source is a string literal and both the source and destination are not null at this point, the lack of explicit bounds checking or capacity information for the destination buffer means there is a real risk of buffer overflow. The verdict for this call site is also marked as genuine, reinforcing the assessment.\n\n## In Context Remediation\nBefore copying data into the destination buffer, ensure that the buffer is large enough to hold the number of bytes being copied. If the size of the destination buffer is not known, pass the buffer size as an additional argument or use a structure that tracks the buffer's length. For example, if `inst` is allocated dynamically, ensure it is at least 3 bytes long before calling the copy operation:\n\n```cpp\n// Example: Ensure inst is allocated with sufficient size\nuint8_t* inst = (uint8_t*)malloc(3);\nif (inst != NULL) {\n    memcpy(inst, \"\\x48\\x81\\xc4\", 3);\n    // ...\n    free(inst);\n}\n```\n\nIf `inst` is a pointer to a buffer provided by the caller, always validate its size before copying. If possible, use safer functions or add explicit checks:\n\n```cpp\n// Example: Add explicit size check\nif (inst_size >= 3) {\n    memcpy(inst, \"\\x48\\x81\\xc4\", 3);\n}\n```\n\n## References\n- [OWASP: Buffer Overflow](https://owasp.org/www-community/vulnerabilities/Buffer_Overflow)\n- [CWE-119: Improper Restriction of Operations within the Bounds of a Memory Buffer](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/119.html)\n- [CWE-120: Buffer Copy without Checking Size of Input ('Classic Buffer Overflow')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/120.html)\n- [C-Based Toolchain Hardening Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n\n___\n"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS121708"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "46b00a57-61fd-36d9-b10c-1b934ece9e3c",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "This finding is classified as a genuine vulnerability because the analysis identified a risk of undefined behavior due to possible overlap between the source and destination memory regions in the memory copy operation. The destination and source may refer to overlapping memory areas, as indicated by the data flow analysis. Additionally, there is no evidence of a guard to prevent this overlap, and the source pointer may also be null, which could lead to a crash or further undefined behavior. The verdict is supported by the high-confidence classification and the explicit 'GENUINE' verdict. These factors indicate a real risk of memory corruption or program instability if the overlap or null pointer dereference occurs.\n\n## In Context Remediation\nTo prevent undefined behavior when copying memory, ensure that the source and destination memory regions do not overlap. If there is any possibility of overlap, use `memmove` instead of `memcpy`, as `memmove` is designed to handle overlapping regions safely. Additionally, check that the source pointer is not null before performing the copy operation.\n\n```cpp\nif (inst != nullptr) {\n    memmove(loc - 4, inst, sizeof(inst));\n}\n```\n\n## References\n- [OWASP C-Based Toolchain Hardening Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-119: Improper Restriction of Operations within the Bounds of a Memory Buffer](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/119.html)\n- [CWE-787: Out-of-bounds Write](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/787.html)\n- [OWASP Buffer Overflow](https://owasp.org/www-community/vulnerabilities/Buffer_Overflow)\n\n___\n"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS121708"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "6c244dfa-69f4-367c-a78d-3fbf576dbe2a",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "This finding is classified as a genuine vulnerability because the analysis identified a risk of undefined behavior due to possible overlap between the source and destination memory regions in the memory copy operation. The destination and source may refer to overlapping memory areas, as indicated by the dataflow analysis. Additionally, there is no evidence of a guard to prevent this overlap, and the source pointer may also be null, which could lead to a crash or further undefined behavior. The verdict is supported by the high-confidence classification and the explicit 'GENUINE' verdict. These factors indicate a real risk of memory corruption or program instability.\n\n## In Context Remediation\nTo prevent undefined behavior when copying memory regions that may overlap, use `memmove` instead of `memcpy`. `memmove` is specifically designed to handle overlapping source and destination regions safely. Replace the original call with:\n\n```cpp\nmemmove(loc - 4, inst, sizeof(inst));\n```\n\nAdditionally, ensure that the source pointer is not null before performing the copy to avoid potential crashes:\n\n```cpp\nif (inst != NULL) {\n    memmove(loc - 4, inst, sizeof(inst));\n}\n```\n\n## References\n- [OWASP: Buffer Overflow](https://owasp.org/www-community/vulnerabilities/Buffer_Overflow)\n- [CWE-119: Improper Restriction of Operations within the Bounds of a Memory Buffer](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/119.html)\n- [CWE-787: Out-of-bounds Write](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/787.html)\n\n\n___\n"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS121708"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "a836acb2-4ffb-364f-8b24-7d94da197e75",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "This finding is considered a genuine vulnerability because the classification indicates a risk of undefined behavior due to possible overlap between the source and destination memory regions in the memory copy operation. The analysis detected that the source and destination may refer to overlapping memory areas, which is not allowed for the memcpy function and can result in unpredictable program behavior. Additionally, there is a risk that the source pointer may be null at this point, as there is no prior check ensuring it is valid. The destination pointer is less likely to be null, but the main concern is the potential for overlapping memory regions. No explicit bounds checks or guards are present, and the size argument is not derived from the destination's capacity. These factors all contribute to the high confidence in this being a real issue.\n\n## In Context Remediation\nTo prevent undefined behavior when the source and destination memory regions may overlap, replace the memory copy operation with a function that safely handles overlapping regions, such as memmove. This ensures that the copy is performed correctly even if the memory areas overlap.\n\n```cpp\nmemmove(buf, inst, sizeof(inst));\n```\n\nAdditionally, ensure that both the source and destination pointers are valid (not null) before performing the operation:\n\n```cpp\nif (buf != nullptr && inst != nullptr) {\n    memmove(buf, inst, sizeof(inst));\n}\n```\n\n## References\n- [OWASP C-Based Toolchain Hardening Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-119: Improper Restriction of Operations within the Bounds of a Memory Buffer](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/119.html)\n- [CWE-787: Out-of-bounds Write](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/787.html)\n- [OWASP Buffer Overflow](https://owasp.org/www-community/vulnerabilities/Buffer_Overflow)\n\n___\n"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS121708"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "5f598648-bcbe-3312-b7e9-3fd6fc8883b6",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "This finding is classified as a genuine vulnerability because the analysis identified a risk of undefined behavior due to possible overlap between the source and destination memory regions in the memory copy operation. The destination and source expressions are both identifiers, and dataflow analysis suggests that the destination buffer may alias or overlap with the source. Additionally, there is no explicit check to ensure that the source pointer is not null, which could also lead to undefined behavior. The destination buffer is a pointer type, and there is no evidence that its capacity is being checked or that the length argument is derived from the destination's size. These factors, combined with the high-confidence classification and verdict, indicate a real risk of memory corruption or program instability.\n\n## In Context Remediation\nTo prevent undefined behavior when copying memory, ensure that the source and destination buffers do not overlap. If there is any possibility of overlap, use `memmove` instead of `memcpy`, as `memmove` is designed to handle overlapping regions safely. Additionally, check that the source pointer is not null before performing the copy to avoid null pointer dereference.\n\n**Remediation Example:**\n\n```cpp\nif (pltData != nullptr && buf != pltData) {\n    memcpy(buf, pltData, sizeof(pltData));\n} else if (pltData != nullptr) {\n    // If overlap is possible, use memmove instead\n    memmove(buf, pltData, sizeof(pltData));\n}\n```\n\n## References\n- [OWASP C-Based Toolchain Hardening Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-119: Improper Restriction of Operations within the Bounds of a Memory Buffer](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/119.html)\n- [CWE-120: Buffer Copy without Checking Size of Input ('Classic Buffer Overflow')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/120.html)\n- [CWE-787: Out-of-bounds Write](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/787.html)\n- [OWASP Buffer Overflow](https://owasp.org/www-community/vulnerabilities/Buffer_Overflow)\n\n___\n"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS121708"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "338696ba-cbb0-3f92-953f-1999ba37ceff",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "This finding is classified as a genuine vulnerability because the analysis identified a risk of undefined behavior due to possible overlap between the source and destination memory regions in the memory copy operation. The destination and source are both pointers, and dataflow analysis suggests that they may refer to overlapping memory areas. Additionally, there is no explicit check to ensure that the source pointer is not null, which could also lead to undefined behavior. The use of a raw memory copy function without overlap protection (such as memcpy) in this context is dangerous, as overlapping regions require special handling. The absence of a guard for null pointers and the potential for overlap make this a high-confidence, actionable issue.\n\n## In Context Remediation\nTo prevent undefined behavior when copying memory regions that may overlap, replace the memory copy function with one that is safe for overlapping regions. In C and C++, `memmove` is designed for this purpose. Replace the original memory copy call with `memmove` to ensure correct behavior even if the source and destination overlap.\n\n```cpp\nmemmove(real, sym, sizeof(SymbolUnion));\n```\n\nAdditionally, ensure that both the source and destination pointers are not null before performing the copy to avoid null pointer dereference:\n\n```cpp\nif (real != nullptr && sym != nullptr) {\n    memmove(real, sym, sizeof(SymbolUnion));\n}\n```\n\n## References\n- [CWE-119: Improper Restriction of Operations within the Bounds of a Memory Buffer](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/119.html)\n- [CWE-787: Out-of-bounds Write](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/787.html)\n- [OWASP: Buffer Overflow](https://owasp.org/www-community/vulnerabilities/Buffer_Overflow)\n- [OWASP C-Based Toolchain Hardening Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n\n___\n"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS121708"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "b87e170f-ce17-39f9-aaea-1dfce8686ee1",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "This finding is classified as a genuine vulnerability because the analysis identified a risk of undefined behavior due to possible overlap between the source and destination memory regions in the memory copy operation. The destination and source are both variables, and dataflow analysis suggests that they may refer to overlapping memory. Additionally, there is no explicit check to ensure that the source is not null, which could also lead to undefined behavior. The destination is a pointer, and there is no evidence that its capacity is being checked or that the length argument is derived from the destination's size. These factors indicate a real risk of memory corruption or program crashes if the overlap or null pointer scenarios occur.\n\n## In Context Remediation\nTo prevent undefined behavior when copying memory, ensure that the source and destination memory regions do not overlap. If there is any possibility of overlap, use `memmove` instead of `memcpy`, as `memmove` is designed to handle overlapping regions safely. Additionally, check that the source pointer is not null before performing the copy to avoid null pointer dereference.\n\n```cpp\nif (buf != nullptr && inst != nullptr) {\n    // Use memmove instead of memcpy if overlap is possible\n    memmove(buf, inst, sizeof(inst));\n}\n```\n\n## References\n- [OWASP: Buffer Overflow](https://owasp.org/www-community/vulnerabilities/Buffer_Overflow)\n- [CWE-119: Improper Restriction of Operations within the Bounds of a Memory Buffer](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/119.html)\n- [CWE-787: Out-of-bounds Write](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/787.html)\n\n\n___\n"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
